@*
    A ruler drawn from the pieces the game draws them from.

    The portrait is not a picture. It is a model wearing three textures — a body, an outfit, and
    whatever is on its head — and the game paints it live. Each of those textures has been drawn
    once per layer of the model, so a figure is those pictures stacked back up in painting order:
    outfit-back, body, outfit-front, head, hair. The order matters, and it is the order the layers
    arrive in; any other puts the coat's back over the chest.

    The rule that makes the stacking work is that a layer is chosen by TEXTURE, never by number.
    It is tempting to think one number per slot indexes every layer of that slot alike, and for most
    portraits it happens to. It is not true: the human male portrait draws its beard from a run of
    forty textures and its hair from a run of eighty-five, so number twelve is two different things
    in the two runs, and a figure picked by number comes out with a black beard under blonde hair.
    A layer that has nothing for the chosen texture simply does not draw — a beard layer against a
    beardless hairstyle — which is exactly right.
*@

<div class="sem-figure" style="width:@(Width)px;height:@(Height)px">
    @foreach (var image in Pieces)
    {
        <img src="@Session.Data.AssetUrl(image.Image)" alt=""
             style="left:@(image.Left)px;top:@(image.Top)px" loading="lazy">
    }
</div>

@code {
    /// <summary>
    /// The frame a portrait is drawn in, which is the size the game's own pictures come out at.
    /// </summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// Every layer's position is recorded against this, so the box has to be exactly it and the
    /// pieces are placed inside rather than scaled to fit. A caller wanting it smaller scales the
    /// whole box.
    /// </remarks>
    private const int Width = 332;

    private const int Height = 220;

    [CascadingParameter]
    public required DesignSession Session { get; set; }

    /// <summary>The portrait's pieces, as extracted.</summary>
    [Parameter, EditorRequired]
    public required PortraitOutfit Outfit { get; set; }

    /// <summary>The body texture to wear, by name.</summary>
    [Parameter]
    public string? Character { get; set; }

    /// <summary>The outfit to wear, by name.</summary>
    [Parameter]
    public string? Clothes { get; set; }

    /// <summary>What is on the head, by name.</summary>
    [Parameter]
    public string? Attachment { get; set; }

    /// <summary>
    /// The pictures to stack, in painting order.
    /// </summary>
    private IEnumerable<PortraitLayerImage> Pieces => Outfit.Layers
        .Select(Piece)
        .OfType<PortraitLayerImage>();

    /// <summary>
    /// Which form of a layer to draw, or none.
    /// </summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// Three cases, and the difference between the last two matters. A layer that has only the one
    /// form the mesh names — written as the texture <c>default</c> — wears it whatever is chosen,
    /// and always draws. A layer that offers real choices draws the one matching the chosen texture,
    /// and where it offers nothing for that texture it draws nothing: a beard layer against a
    /// beardless hairstyle is meant to be absent, not to fall back to some other beard. And a slot
    /// with no choice made at all shows its first form, which is what the empire designer shows,
    /// since the extractor writes each layer's forms with the designer's own first.
    /// </remarks>
    private PortraitLayerImage? Piece(PortraitLayer layer)
    {
        if (layer.Images is [{ Texture: "default" } only])
        {
            return only;
        }

        return Chosen(layer) is { } wanted
            ? layer.Images.FirstOrDefault(i => string.Equals(i.Texture, wanted, StringComparison.OrdinalIgnoreCase))
            : layer.Images.FirstOrDefault();
    }

    private string? Chosen(PortraitLayer layer) => layer.Slot switch
    {
        PortraitSlot.Clothes => Clothes,
        PortraitSlot.Attachment => Attachment,
        _ => Character,
    };
}
